WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.040
In August 2017, Denver Public Schools
unveiled a new school lunch policy in a

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.490
push to end lunch shaming. There are
students who are eligible for the

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.180
federal free and reduced lunch program – set
them aside. They're not the ones we're

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.869
talking about. There are students who are
not eligible for federal free and reduced

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:26.189
lunch because their families can afford to
pay for lunch. What Denver said to these

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:30.060
families is that their children would
get lunch at school if they paid for it,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.940
and they would get lunch if they did not.
A year later, the check came due. Debt from

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.980
unpaid lunches in the 2017-18 school
year grew more than 25 fold, to more

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.340
than $350,000, or about nine hundred unpaid

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.450
lunches a day. Now, no one wants to see
children go hungry because of their

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.440
family's financial circumstances. But
neither do we want to shift dollars from

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.489
academics to the lunch line due to
feel-good schemes that go south. The

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:01.140
lesson: Even the most well-meaning of
bureaucratic policies can have unintended

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:07.180
consequences, eroding the trust essential
to healthy schools and compassionate communities.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.200
Is it surprising to you that
so many people seemed to take advantage of

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.930
Denver's program? And why or why not? Let
us know in your comments. Also, let us

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:23.939
know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:30.860
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

